\section{Effectiveness and Counterfactuals}
\label{sec:effectiveness}

A remedy is useful only if it would have stopped real failures. Table~\ref{tab:coverage}
crosses all twenty-eight corpus incidents with the fourteen remedy families. We first state the
rules that produce its cells, then read it by column, asking what each remedy is good for, and by
row, asking whether a preventing remedy existed when each system shipped.

\input{sections/coverage_table}

\subsection{Grading rules}
\label{sec:coverage-rules}
A cell is \hbna{} when the remedy acts on no stage the incident touches, with stage sets from
Tables~\ref{tab:incidents} and~\ref{tab:remedies}. Otherwise it is \hbfull{} when the remedy,
deployed as specified, removes the specific cause; \hbhalf{} when it detects the failure or limits
its blast radius without removing the cause; and \hbnone{} when it would not have changed the
outcome. R8 is graded by rule rather than case by case: \hbhalf{} when the incident is
statistically observable and \hbnone{} otherwise, since an output test can only flag output that
fails it. The other \hbfull{} and \hbhalf{} judgments are made per incident from its primary
sources and recorded with a one-line reason in the released data. Availability refers to the date
the vulnerable system shipped: Y means the preventing remedy was published and implemented in
mainstream libraries, standards, or platforms; P that it was published but not standardized, not
the default, or not available for that platform; E that it had been proposed without a production
implementation; N that it had not been proposed in deployable form. The generating script checks
that every judgment respects the stage rule. These rules make the judgments explicit and
checkable rather than objective: a reader who disagrees with a cell can change it and regenerate
every count reported here.

\subsection{Reading by column: what each remedy is good for}
Two columns carry the argument. R8, statistical testing and certification, is what industry
deploys most widely, and it prevents none of the twenty-eight failures. It detects two, MIFARE and
the \texttt{RDSEED} zeros, and against the silent majority, nineteen of twenty-eight, it has no
effect even where it applies. R9, build-path verification, is the only family that prevents a
failure on the build and integration surface, and it prevents all three corpus incidents that
touch it, Debian, Juniper, and \coldcard{}. R13, verifiable entropy at key generation, also
reaches \stage{$\ast$} but can at most detect. Among the remaining columns, prevention is
concentrated: a vetted CSPRNG (R2) prevents nine incidents, robust seeding (R3) eight, hedged
nonces (R5) four, and reseeding on lifecycle events (R4), a vetted source (R1), and formal
verification (R11) three each. Six families prevent nothing in the corpus. Hedging (R6), user
entropy (R10), and verifiable entropy (R13) contain or detect many failures, eight to ten each,
without removing their causes, and anti-exfil signing (R14) targets nonce exfiltration, which has
been demonstrated (Appendix~\ref{sec:covert}) but does not appear among deployed incidents. A
fourteen-family toolkit is therefore, in practice, seven preventive families, one of which alone
guards the build, and a layer of detection and containment.

\subsection{Reading by row: the remedy usually existed}
Twenty-four incidents have a preventing remedy in the catalog, and in twenty-two of them that
remedy was available at least in part when the system shipped: fully in fifteen, partly in seven.
The fifteen are mostly failures of defaults. A general-purpose generator was the convenient
choice in MIFARE, the PHP token generators, Trust Wallet, Milk Sad, and Ill Bloom. Non-blocking
early reads were the default behind Mining Ps and Qs. Random rather than deterministic nonces
were the library default behind the Android and biased-nonce failures. Randomness failures in this
corpus are, for the most part, not a research gap but a gap between available and deployed
defenses. The exceptions are instructive. The only incident with no remedy yet proposed is the
Debian patch of 2008, a \stage{$\ast$} failure, and \coldcard{} and Juniper, the other two
\stage{$\ast$} failures, had only partial remedies: reproducible builds existed, but nothing bound
the shipped binary to the intended source. The four incidents with no preventing remedy at all,
the CTR\_DRBG cache attack, Minerva, TPM-Fail, and ROCA, mark the edge of a randomness catalog:
they need constant-time implementations or vetted key-generation algorithms, which belong to
implementation security rather than to randomness generation.

\subsection{Access, observability, and impact}
\label{sec:economics}
Counterfactuals say which remedy would have helped. Which failures an adversary actually pursues
depends on what exploitation costs, and the corpus attributes of Table~\ref{tab:incidents} show a
consistent pattern. Eighteen of the twenty-eight incidents are exploitable from public data alone
(A1). Thirteen of these are silent and twelve reach a whole ecosystem. The seven incidents that
need local access (A2) include four of the five statistically observable ones, and the three
supply-chain incidents (A3) are the \dualec{} cases. The cheapest failures to exploit are thus
also the hardest to see and the widest in reach, and for a silent A1 failure the first external
symptom is the exploitation itself. Defense in such cases must be preventive rather than
reactive, which returns the argument to the preventive columns of the matrix.

\subsection{Certification tracked the wrong property}
Three incidents, \dualec{}, DUHK, and \coldcard{}, involved products that were certified,
audited, or marketed on the strength of their randomness, and all three failed silently.
Certification measured statistical quality and process conformance, which a predictable but
uniform generator, or a build that bypasses the certified component, does not
violate~\cite{checkoway2014dualec,cohney2018duhk,coinkite2026backgrounder}. The matrix suggests
what an assurance regime should require in addition: a power-on known-answer test of the real
entropy path that fails closed, a reproducible build that binds reviewed source to the shipped
binary, and, where the primitive allows, deterministic or hedged constructions that take
randomness out of the security argument. None of these is new. The contribution of the matrix is
the evidence, incident by incident, that these are the defenses that track the failures.
